WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:09.882
For discrete variables, Bayesian networks often use conditional probability tables, or CPTs. A discrete variable has a set of named states, such as true

2
00:00:09.882 --> 00:00:13.176
or false, high or low, working or failed.

3
00:00:13.176 --> 00:00:18.529
A root node has no parents, so it uses a prior probability distribution.

4
00:00:18.529 --> 00:00:25.118
For example, Weather might have probabilities for sunny, cloudy, and rainy before considering any parent variable.

5
00:00:25.118 --> 00:00:28.824
A child node has probabilities conditioned on its parents.

6
00:00:28.824 --> 00:00:34.176
Suppose a simple network has Weather and Sprinkler as parents of Wet Ground.

7
00:00:34.176 --> 00:00:41.588
The probability of Wet Ground depends on whether the weather is rainy and whether the sprinkler is on.

8
00:00:41.588 --> 00:00:47.765
The CPT for Wet Ground has a row for each relevant combination of parent states.

9
00:00:47.765 --> 00:00:51.059
Each row should form a valid probability distribution.

10
00:00:51.059 --> 00:00:58.059
In a true-or-false row, the probability of true plus the probability of false should sum to one.

11
00:00:58.059 --> 00:01:03.000
The graph lets the joint probability distribution be factored into local terms.

12
00:01:03.000 --> 00:01:12.882
One compact way to write the idea is: the probability of all variables together equals the product of each variable's probability given its parents.

13
00:01:12.882 --> 00:01:22.765
In words, instead of building one enormous table that lists every possible state combination, the Bayesian network breaks the model into smaller local relationships.

14
00:01:22.765 --> 00:01:27.294
That is one reason Bayesian networks are useful for practical modeling.

15
00:01:27.294 --> 00:01:29.353
Factorization is not magic compression.

16
00:01:29.353 --> 00:01:33.471
It depends on the conditional independence assumptions in the graph.

17
00:01:33.471 --> 00:01:39.235
If the graph leaves out an important dependency, the factorized model can be misleading.

18
00:01:39.235 --> 00:01:49.118
Also, not every Bayesian network uses simple CPTs. Continuous or hybrid networks may use Gaussian distributions, regression-style conditional models, or other local probability distributions.

19
00:01:49.118 --> 00:01:56.118
The core idea remains the same: local distributions, organized by the graph, define the larger probabilistic model.

20
00:01:56.118 --> 00:01:59.824
In practice, CPT design is often a modeling bottleneck.

21
00:01:59.824 --> 00:02:07.647
Too many parents can create a table with many rows, and each row needs probability values that mean something.

22
00:02:07.647 --> 00:02:14.235
Modelers sometimes simplify states, combine variables, or use structured conditional distributions to keep the model manageable.

23
00:02:14.235 --> 00:02:18.353
The goal is not to make the largest possible table.

24
00:02:18.353 --> 00:02:25.353
The goal is to represent the relationships at the level of detail needed for the reasoning question.
